Deliberated document format and presentation strategy

Here's the full architecture design. Key decisions worth calling out:

Altitude 360000 is deliberate — it places DVault in the FSFilter Activity Monitor load order group, which puts it above antivirus drivers (~260000). That means if an AV quarantine-deletes a file, DVault captures it first. If you were building this for your forensic use case you'd want this exact ordering.

Two delete paths are both covered:

    IRP_MJ_SET_INFORMATION / FileDispositionInformation[Ex] — the standard DeleteFile() path
    IRP_MJ_CLEANUP with DeletePending flag — the DeleteOnClose handle path that many tools miss

The async copy problem is noted in the limitations table — for large files you cannot block the calling process in pre-op while doing a synchronous 100GB copy. The real implementation needs a kernel work item queue that copies asynchronously, immediately replies success to the IRP, and then does the copy. The delete will proceed but the vault copy races to finish before the NTFS deallocate completes (which it usually wins at typical I/O speeds).

Scenario B (ransomware) is where this architecture genuinely shines over any userland solution — by the time ransomware has encrypted and deleted a file, your minifilter already has a pristine pre-encryption copy in the vault.

The INF has StartType = SERVICE_BOOT_START so the filter loads before any user processes, closing the early-boot deletion window.
